export default "## What Is Implantation Bleeding\n\nImplantation bleeding is light spotting that some people experience when a fertilized egg attaches to the uterine lining. During implantation, small blood vessels in the endometrium can be disrupted, producing a small amount of bleeding that makes its way out of the cervix.\n\nNot everyone experiences it. Many people who become pregnant notice no spotting at implantation at all. Light spotting is also easy to miss or attribute to something else. Its presence does not confirm pregnancy, and its absence does not indicate a problem.\n\nThis guide is for educational purposes only and is not a substitute for medical advice.\n\n## When Does It Happen\n\nImplantation typically occurs 6 to 12 days after ovulation. In a 28-day cycle where ovulation happens on day 14, this places possible implantation bleeding between roughly days 20 and 26. That window often overlaps with when premenstrual spotting might also occur, which is one reason the two are frequently confused.\n\nThe timing relative to your expected period matters:\n\n- Implantation bleeding typically appears a few days before an expected period\n- It rarely starts on the exact expected period start date\n- If spotting appears right on cue with your normal period timing and then continues for several days, it is more likely to be menstruation\n\n## How to Recognize Implantation Bleeding\n\n### Color\n\nImplantation bleeding is characteristically pink or light brown rather than the bright or dark red typical of menstrual flow. The color comes from blood that has taken time to travel through the cervix. Older blood oxidizes and turns brown.\n\n### Flow\n\nThe volume is the most distinguishing feature. Implantation bleeding is light, usually nothing more than a few spots on underwear, or pink-tinged discharge when wiping. Most people do not need a pad or tampon. Anything requiring period protection is more likely to be a period.\n\n### Duration\n\nImplantation bleeding usually lasts a few hours to about two days. A typical period lasts anywhere from three to seven days with progressively changing flow. If spotting continues for three or more days with increasing volume, that pattern fits menstruation more closely.\n\n### Cramping\n\nSome people notice mild cramps around the time of implantation, but severe or one-sided pain is not a feature of implantation bleeding. Significant pelvic pain alongside spotting in early pregnancy warrants prompt medical evaluation to rule out ectopic pregnancy.\n\n## How It Differs From a Period\n\n| Feature | Implantation Bleeding | Period |\n|---|---|---|\n| Color | Pink or brown | Red, darkening over time |\n| Flow | Very light, spotting only | Light to heavy |\n| Duration | Hours to 2 days | 3 to 7 days |\n| Cramping | Mild or none | Variable, often more pronounced |\n| Timing | A few days before expected period | On or near expected date |\n| Clots | None | Possible |\n\nNo single feature definitively separates implantation bleeding from a light period. The combination of color, flow, and duration, considered alongside your cycle history, gives you the most information.\n\n## What to Track\n\nIf you experience unusual spotting and want to document it:\n\n- **Date and cycle day:** note where in your cycle the spotting occurred\n- **Color:** pink, brown, or red\n- **Volume:** spots only, or heavier\n- **Duration:** how many hours or days\n- **Associated symptoms:** any cramping, breast tenderness, nausea, or other changes\n- **Recent sexual activity and contraceptive use:** relevant context for a healthcare provider\n\nTracking this data over time helps you distinguish your personal cycle patterns from one-off variations. If implantation bleeding is something you experience in pregnancies, you may begin to recognize it as a distinct pattern in your logs.\n\n## When to Take a Pregnancy Test\n\nA home pregnancy test is the practical next step when implantation bleeding is possible. However, timing matters.\n\nMost tests are calibrated to detect hCG from the first day of a missed period. Testing during the spotting itself, several days before the expected period, risks a false negative because hCG levels are still rising and may be below the detection threshold.\n\nIf you suspect implantation bleeding, wait until your period is expected. If it does not arrive, test then. If you get a negative result but your period still does not arrive within a few more days, test again. Early tests can miss pregnancies when hCG is rising slowly.\n\n## When to Seek Medical Attention\n\nContact a healthcare provider if you experience:\n\n- Spotting during a confirmed pregnancy at any stage\n- Heavy bleeding that does not resolve as expected\n- Significant one-sided pelvic or abdominal pain alongside spotting\n- Spotting accompanied by shoulder pain or dizziness (which can indicate internal bleeding)\n- A positive pregnancy test followed by heavy bleeding\n\nLight mid-cycle spotting without other symptoms and unrelated to pregnancy is generally not an emergency, but any unexplained change in your bleeding pattern is worth discussing with a provider.\n\n## A Note on Tracking Reproductive Health Data Privately\n\nSpotting, cycle timing, and pregnancy-adjacent symptoms are among the most sensitive health data a person can generate. If you log potential implantation bleeding in a period tracking app, that data, including inferred pregnancy status, is stored somewhere.\n\nFlo Health, one of the largest period tracking apps, reached an FTC enforcement action in 2021 for sharing period and health data with Facebook and Google. A $59.5 million class action settlement followed in 2025. Period tracker data is not covered by HIPAA. In states where abortion is criminalized, health data including cycle records and pregnancy-related spotting can be subpoenaed.\n\nApps that store readable data on company servers, regardless of their privacy claims, can be compelled to hand over that data. Floriva keeps core records on-device and has no readable central cycle database to produce. Drip and Euki are local-only options. Device access remains a separate legal path.\n\nIf reproductive privacy matters to you, where your tracking data lives is worth thinking about before you log it.";
